  "subjects_top_term": "Agriculture and food",
  "summary": {
    "as": "Introduced in House",
    "asOf": "2025-07-23",
    "date": "2026-11-17T16:11:15Z",
    "text": "Protecting Our Farms and Homes from China Act\n\nThis bill prohibits certain foreign\u00a0entities and individuals\u00a0connected\u00a0to China or the Chinese government from (1) acquiring, leasing, or owning any interest in U.S. agricultural land; or (2) purchasing or owning U.S. residential real estate for two years (with an option for the President to extend the prohibition).\n\nSpecifically, these prohibitions apply to certain foreign individuals and entities with connections to China, including (1) a business incorporated in China; (2) a person, company, university, or organization that can legally enter into contracts, own properties, or pay taxes on behalf of the Chinese government; and (3) an individual or organization affiliated with the Chinese Communist Party.\n\nA\u00a0 covered entity or individual must\n\ndivest\u00a0from any ownership or leased interest in U.S. agricultural land within one year of the bill's enactment,sign a letter of intent to divest from interests in the agricultural land within 180 days of enactment,\u00a0anddivest from any\u00a0ownership of U.S.\u00a0residential real estate\u00a0within one year of the bill's enactment.The bill establishes civil and criminal penalties for violations.\n\nFurther, for violations involving agricultural land, the bill (1) provides for property forfeiture and sale by public auction, and (2) nullifies any noncompete agreement between a covered foreign entity or individual with an ownership or leased interest in U.S. agricultural land and an employee of the entity.\n\nFinally, the Department of Agriculture and the Department of Commerce must establish offices to monitor compliance and impose civil fines."
  },
